\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 Oscar sat a sturdy, round wooden table back in the library.}
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 My brain wants there to be an 'at' after sat or some other coordinating preposition. It feels unnatural for sat to be followed by the object he sat at without that. *sturdy, round wooden - three adjectives to describe the table. Are any of these important? Adjectives can help make prose descriptive but too many and it becomes difficult to know what to pay attention to. Should I care that the table is sturdy because Oscar is going to stand up and fight on it later? Or that it's wooden because he's going to break it into pieces to start a fire? Or that it's round because he won't rip his head open on a corner when he later falls on it? I know those were absurd questions because I'm not sure you're going to turn this into a fight scene. Dude's just sitting here reading. I mention it to point out the words you use to describe things tell me something as a reader. What story can I expect? What kind of character is this? What should I be picturing in my head?}
