\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b1 Save the Cat / beat sheets}{ These are templates for story pacing. They break a story into \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93beats\u8221\'94 like Setup \u8594\'3F Catalyst \u8594\'3F Debate \u8594\'3F Midpoint \u8594\'3F Finale. The idea: if your story hits these beats in the right rhythm, readers stay engaged.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b1 Motivation/Reaction Units (MRUs)}{ A micro\_level writing tool. It says:}
\par\pard\plain \li540\fi-270\sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch \ls1\ilvl0{\listtext	 \uc0\u8226 	}{First something }{\f0\fs24\i1 happens}{ (motivation).}
\par\plain \ilvl0{\listtext	 \uc0\u8226 	}{Then the character }{\f0\fs24\i1 reacts}{ (reaction). If you keep this order clear, scenes feel natural and immersive.}
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b1 Scene/Sequel structure}{ A classic structure from Dwight Swain.}
\par\pard\plain \li540\fi-270\sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch \ls2\ilvl0{\listtext	 \uc0\u8226 	}{\f0\fs24\b1 Scene}{ = Goal \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8594\'3F Conflict \u8594\'3F Disaster}
\par\plain \ilvl0{\listtext	 \uc0\u8226 	}{\f0\fs24\b1 Sequel}{ = Reaction \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8594\'3F Dilemma \u8594\'3F Decision This creates a chain of cause-and-effect that keeps the story moving.}}